      "output": "**Water (H₂O)** is a simple yet remarkably versatile chemical compound that is essential for life and the planet’s climate. Here’s a concise overview:\n\n| Aspect | Details |\n|--------|---------|\n| **Chemical formula** | H₂O – two hydrogen atoms covalently bonded to one oxygen atom. |\n| **Molecular structure** | Bent shape (≈104.5° bond angle) giving it a polar, “V‑shaped” geometry. |\n| **Polarity** | The oxygen atom is more electronegative, pulling electron density toward itself. This creates a partial negative charge near oxygen and partial positive charges near hydrogens. |\n| **Hydrogen bonding** | The polarity allows each water molecule to form up to four hydrogen bonds (two as donors, two as acceptors). This network gives water many unique properties. |\n| **Physical states** | Solid (ice), liquid (water), gas (vapor). The transitions occur at 0 °C (freezing) and 100 °C (boiling) under standard atmospheric pressure. |\n| **Key properties** | • **High specific heat** – absorbs heat without large temperature changes, moderating climates. <br>• **High surface tension** – supports small objects and drives capillary action. <br>• **Excellent solvent** – dissolves many ionic and polar substances, enabling biochemical reactions. <br>• **Density anomaly** – ice is less dense than liquid water, allowing it to float and insulate aquatic life in winter. |\n| **Biological role** | • **Solvent for cells** – carries nutrients, wastes, and signaling molecules. <br>• **Medium for metabolic reactions** – many biochemical pathways occur in aqueous environments. <br>• **Regulates temperature** – through sweating, respiration, and circulation. |\n| **Environmental role** | • **Hydrologic cycle** – evaporation, condensation, precipitation, runoff, and groundwater recharge. <br>• **Climate regulator** – absorbs and re‑emits infrared radiation, influencing global temperatures. |\n| **Common uses** | • Drinking, cooking, sanitation. <br>• Industrial processes (cooling, chemical synthesis, power generation). <br>• Agriculture (irrigation). <br>• Energy (hydropower, fuel cells). |\n\nIn short, water is a polar, hydrogen‑bonding liquid that exists naturally in all three states, making it indispensable for life, ecosystems, and human technology. Its unique combination of chemical and physical properties—especially its ability to dissolve a wide range of substances and its role in heat regulation—makes it the “universal solvent” and a cornerstone of Earth’s systems."
